Important Git commands to remember for practical
Purpose	Command
Initialize	git init
Clone	git clone URL
Status	git status
Add all files	git add .
Commit	git commit -m "message"
Push	git push origin main
Pull	git pull origin main
Fetch	git fetch origin
Create branch	git checkout -b branch-name
Switch branch	git switch branch-name
Merge	git merge branch-name
View branches	git branch -a
View history	git log --oneline
Resolve conflict stage	git add .
Stash	git stash
Restore stash	git stash pop
Apply patch	git apply fix.patch




Part A — Create Maven Java Project in Eclipse
Step 1: Open Eclipse
Open Eclipse and select your workspace.
Step 2: Create Maven Project
Go to:

File → New → Project → Maven → Maven Project → Next
Step 3: Choose workspace
Keep the default workspace location and click Next.
Step 4: Choose Archetype
Select:

maven-archetype-quickstart

Version: 1.4

Click Next.
Step 5: Enter project details
Field	Value
Group ID	com.example
Artifact ID	my-maven-project
Version	Default
Package	com.example

Click Finish.
Step 6: Project structure
You should see:
my-maven-project
├── src/main/java
├── src/test/java
└── pom.xml


Step 7: Update project
Right-click project → Maven → Update Project → Finish.
Step 8: Build commands in Eclipse
Right-click project and select:
- Run As → Maven Clean
- Run As → Maven Install
- Run As → Maven Test
- Run As → Maven Build
Step 9: Maven Build
Right-click project → Run As → Maven Build.

In Goals, type:
clean install test


Click Apply → Run.
Step 10: Check output
In Console, check:
BUILD SUCCESS


Step 11: Run application
Right-click App.java → Run As → Java Application.

Expected output:
Hello World!


Part B — Create Maven Web Project
Step 1
File → New → Project → Maven → Maven Project → Next
Step 2
Select archetype:
maven-archetype-webapp


Click Next.
Step 3: Project details
Field	Value
Group ID	com.example
Artifact ID	my-web-app

Click Finish.
Step 4: Add Servlet dependency
Open pom.xml and add inside <dependencies>:
<dependency>
    <groupId>javax.servlet</groupId>
    <artifactId>javax.servlet-api</artifactId>
    <version>4.0.1</version>
    <scope>provided</scope>
</dependency>


Step 5: Configure Tomcat
In Eclipse:

Window → Show View → Servers

Then:

Add Server → Apache Tomcat v9.0 → Next

Select Tomcat installation directory and Finish.
Step 6: Build Web Project
Right-click project → Run As → Maven Build.

Goals:
clean install test


Click Apply → Run.
Step 7: Run on Tomcat
Right-click index.jsp → Run As → Run on Server.

Select Tomcat → Finish.
Step 8: Check output
You should see your Hello World webpage in the browser.

Part C — Important Maven commands
Run these in the project directory containing pom.xml:
mvn test


mvn clean install


mvn package


mvn clean


mvn dependency:tree


mvn -X



Part D — Compiler Plugin
Add this inside the pom.xml <build><plugins> section:
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
    <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>3.11.0</version>
    <configuration>
        <source>17</source>
        <target>17</target>
    </configuration>
</plugin>


Run:
mvn package


Inspect the generated JAR:
jar tf target/myapp.jar



Part E — JUnit Testing
Create your test file under:
src/test/java


Run:
mvn test


Check test results:
target/test-classes/
target/surefire-reports/



Part F — Resource Files
Create:
src/main/resources/config.properties


After building, check:
target/classes/


The resource file should be copied there.

Part G — Executable JAR
Run:
mvn package


Then:
java -jar target/myapp.jar



Part H — WAR file
In pom.xml:
<packaging>war</packaging>


Project structure:
src/main/webapp/
└── WEB-INF/
    └── web.xml


Build:
mvn package


Your WAR will be generated inside:
target/
